code-review-report.md:32: #### `isTerminalStatus` not imported — auto-advance always fires
code-review-report.md:38: `getActiveFlightsForDate()` called `isTerminalStatus(f.status)` which was a `ReferenceError` at runtime (never imported). The `try/catch` swallowed the error and returned `[]`, causing the auto-advance logic to always conclude "all flights completed" and show tomorrow's schedule. Every page load with flights today + tomorrow would incorrectly advance.
code-review-report.md:40: **Remediation:** Added `isTerminalStatus` to re-exports in `$lib/server/db.ts` and to the import destructure in `+page.server.ts`.
code-review-report.md:52: The `STATUS_PRIORITY` map had `Landed:50`, `Cancelled:50`, `Diverted:50` but not `Completed`. A flight with status "Completed" would get priority 0 (default), meaning `isTerminalStatus` returned `false` — it was treated as still active. This would prevent auto-advance even when all flights are genuinely completed with that status.
code-review-report.md:88: Both components manually checked `status.includes('landed') || status.includes('completed')` and similar patterns instead of using the shared `isTerminalStatus` or `isFlightCompleted` functions. These hardcoded patterns drift from the canonical terminal status definitions.
code-review-report.md:96: #### `fr24-scraper` imports `isTerminalStatus` from two different packages
code-review-report.md:102: `scraper.ts` imports `isTerminalStatus` from `@airways/database` (priority-based) while `index.ts` imports `isTerminalStatus` (and `TERMINAL_STATUSES`) from `@airways/common` (exact-match). These two implementations have subtly different behavior — e.g. the common version handles `diverted` prefix matching while the database version only matches exact "Diverted". Both are used in the same scraper process.
